# 4. Indexing plan

Indexes must follow real query patterns. Do not add every possible index.

## High-value indexes

```sql
-- webhook idempotency
create unique index on processed_messages(provider_message_id);
create unique index on webhook_events(provider_event_id);

-- customer lookup
create unique index on customers(phone_number);
create index on customer_addresses(customer_id, is_default);

-- active baskets and carts
create index on baskets(customer_id, status, updated_at desc);
create index on basket_lines(basket_id, position);
create index on carts(customer_id, status, expires_at);

-- order operations
create index on orders(vendor_id, status, created_at desc);
create index on orders(customer_id, created_at desc);
create index on orders(driver_id, status, created_at desc);
create index on orders(status, vendor_confirmation_deadline);

-- event timeline
create index on order_events(order_id, created_at);

-- notification queue
create index on notification_outbox(status, next_attempt_at)
where status in ('queued', 'retry');
create index on notification_attempts(order_id, created_at desc);

-- driver dispatch
create index on driver_assignments(zone_id, status, created_at desc);
create index on driver_location_events(order_id, captured_at desc);

-- menu search
create index on menu_items(vendor_id, available, section_id);
create index on menu_items using gin(search_vector);
```

Confirm every important query with:

```sql
explain (analyze, buffers)
```

Do not create indexes without checking write overhead and query plans.

## Partial indexes

Use partial indexes for active records:

```sql
create index on orders(vendor_id, created_at desc)
where status in ('pending_vendor_confirmation', 'accepted', 'preparing', 'ready_for_pickup');
```

This keeps hot indexes smaller.

## Avoid offset pagination

For menus, orders, and events use cursor/keyset pagination:

```text
where created_at < last_seen_created_at
order by created_at desc
limit 20
```

Avoid large `offset` values in active operational tables.

