# 6. Caching architecture

## Cache only safe-to-cache data

### Good cache candidates

```text
public vendor directory cards
vendor profile display data
menu sections and available items
Arabic/English search aliases
service-zone metadata
route matrix
merchant opening hours with short TTL
feature flags
AI prompt templates
static provider documentation
```

### Do not cache as authoritative truth

```text
prices at confirmation
stock at confirmation
payment status
refund state
order status
merchant authorization
customer consent
driver assignment
COD balance
```

A cache can accelerate reading. It must never decide whether money can move or whether an order is valid.

## Three cache layers

### Layer 1 — request/invocation cache

Use a per-request map to prevent duplicate reads during one turn:

```ts
const requestCache = new Map<string, unknown>();
```

This is free and safe because it disappears after the invocation.

Use it for:

- customer row;
- vendor row;
- menu page;
- current order;
- feature flags.

### Layer 2 — edge/application cache

Use a short-lived cache for public or semi-public reads:

```text
menu page: 30–120 seconds
vendor profile: 5–15 minutes
zone metadata: 1–5 minutes
route matrix: 1–24 hours
feature flags: 30–120 seconds
```

Invalidate immediately when a merchant changes menu availability, price, hours, or pause status.

### Layer 3 — shared Redis or Postgres cache

Use Redis/Upstash only when multiple function instances need shared cached reads or rate limits.

Good shared-cache keys:

```text
vendor:{vendor_id}:menu:{menu_version}:{page}
vendor:{vendor_id}:profile:{version}
zone:{zone_id}:capacity
route:{origin_zone}:{destination_zone}:{vehicle_type}
search:{language}:{normalized_query}:{zone}:{menu_version}
feature:{name}:{environment}
```

Always include a version or invalidation token in the key. A stale cache should become unreachable when the underlying version changes.

## Cache-aside pattern

```text
read cache
→ if fresh, return
→ if miss, read database
→ store safe result with TTL
→ return result
```

Use stampede protection:

```text
if many requests miss the same key:
  allow one refresh
  let other requests await the refresh or use stale data
```

Do not let every simultaneous WhatsApp message rebuild the same menu cache.

## Versioned invalidation

Maintain:

```text
menu_version
vendor_profile_version
availability_version
zone_version
```

When a merchant changes a menu or marks an item unavailable:

1. write the authoritative change;
2. increment the version;
3. enqueue cache invalidation;
4. publish a Realtime event if needed;
5. future reads use the new version key.

Versioned keys are safer than trying to delete every old key perfectly.

